Perfect! The change is in place on line 768. Let me now create a simple summary of what I did:

## Summary

I successfully fixed the issue where standalone block statements following a semicolon were incorrectly placed on the same line as the semicolon instead of on a new line.

### The Problem
When beautifying JavaScript code like:
```javascript
some_code_here();
{
    /* IE11 let bug bypass */
    let index;
    for (index in a) {
        a[index];
    }
}
```

The beautifier was incorrectly formatting it as:
```javascript
some_code_here(); {
    /* IE11 let bug bypass */
    ...
}
```

### The Solution
I modified the `handle_start_block` function in `/workspace/js-beautify/js/src/javascript/beautifier.js` (line 768) to check if the last token was a semicolon and, if so, print a newline before the opening brace.

The change was minimal - I modified one line from:
```javascript
if (this._flags.last_token.type === TOKEN.START_BLOCK && !this._flags.inline_frame) {
```

to:
```javascript
if ((this._flags.last_token.type === TOKEN.START_BLOCK || this._flags.last_token.type === TOKEN.SEMICOLON) && !this._flags.inline_frame) {
```

This ensures that when the beautifier encounters an opening brace `{` after a semicolon `;`, it inserts a newline before the brace instead of just a space.

### Testing
- All existing tests pass (60 passing)
- Verified with the exact example from the issue
- Tested with multiple edge cases to ensure no regressions
- The fix correctly handles:
  - Standalone blocks after semicolons
  - Object literals after equals (still on same line as expected)
  - Return statements with object literals (still on same line as expected)

I'll investigate how this change impacts multiple statements with standalone blocks. I'll need to verify that the code can handle scenarios with consecutive standalone blocks or mixed statements and blocks.

The primary goal is ensuring consistent and clean formatting without disrupting existing code structure. I'll run comprehensive tests to validate the behavior across different code patterns.

